<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grandes Heures of Anne of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1503–1508</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grandes Heures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a> is an illuminated book of hours painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bourdichon">Jean Bourdichon</a> for Anne of Brittany, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in about 1503–1508. Its full-page miniatures and its borders of more than 300 plants, painted with their French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names, make it one of the masterpieces of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> illumination. It is kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">Bibliothèque nationale de France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grandes-heures-of-anne-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Grandes Heures of Anne of Brittany" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Bourdichon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BNF_-_Latin_9474_-_Jean_Bourdichon_-_Grandes_Heures_d%27Anne_de_Bretagne_-_f._3r_-_Anne_de_Bretagne_entre_trois_saintes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>, the last independent ruler of Brittany, was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> twice, as the wife of Charles VIII and then of his successor Louis XII. Jean Bourdichon, one of the main artists working regularly for the court, had already illuminated the Louis XII Book of Hours for the king, probably from 1498, and the queen's book was on a still more ambitious scale. It was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> or perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. According to a letter from the queen of March 1508, Bourdichon was paid 1,500 livres tournois in the form of 600 écus, although the payment seems not to have been made for several years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book is large for a book of hours, 30.5 by 20 centimetres, with 476 pages. Besides the 49 full-page miniatures it has 12 calendar pages with scenes of the months, two pages of Anne's heraldic devices and 337 pages with illuminated borders. The miniatures draw on both Italian and Flemish painting, with well-developed perspective, gold highlights learned from Bourdichon's master Jean Fouquet, and painted frames imitating gilded wood. Borrowings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perugino">Perugino</a> suggest he may have made an unrecorded visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>A donor portrait shows Anne at prayer, presented by her patron saints to a Pietà on the facing page. There are night scenes of the Annunciation to the Shepherds and the Nativity, the martyrdoms of Saint Ursula's companions and the Theban Legion, and the rare scene of the Virgin taught to read by Saint Anne. The borders usually show a single species of plant, often with its roots and bulbs, among insects and small animals such as snails, lizards and squirrels, each labelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and French in the manner of a herbal. The plants include Cannabis sativa and the main cereal crops of the day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After Anne's death in 1514 the book remained with the French royal family until the Revolution and then passed to the government; it is catalogued as Ms lat. 9474. Some 395 of its images are available online through the library. Because its miniatures resemble framed panel paintings, many art historians have seen it as foreshadowing the decline of book illustration, and Anthony Blunt wrote that some of its designs look like altarpieces rather than miniatures. Giulio Camus described its plants in 1894, and a full facsimile was published in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grandes Heures of Anne of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
